# Line endings — why this file exists
#
# Windows ends its lines with two characters where the rest of the world uses
# only one. By default Git converts them when checking files out on a Windows
# machine, which is convenient for text and disastrous here.
#
# The sources vendored in `vendor/` come with a SHA-256 hash per file, which
# cargo checks before any build. A line-ending conversion changes the file,
# hence its hash, hence cargo refuses:
#
#     error: the listed checksum of `vendor\ml-dsa\src\lib.rs` has changed
#
# The Windows build thus failed in forty seconds, while the other three
# succeeded. It is a defect that can only show up on a single platform, and
# only once the project has gone through a repository.

# Everywhere: Unix line endings, whatever the system.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Vendored sources: no conversion, ever. Their hash depends on it.
vendor/** -text

# Windows command files want Windows line endings.
# A `.bat` with LF often works, and fails in confusing ways as soon as it
# contains a label or an `if (...)` block.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# And the macOS launcher wants the opposite, or the shell reads one character
# too many on every line.
*.command text eol=lf

# Binary files, for the record.
*.png binary
*.gz binary
*.zip binary
